Refresh the packaged reference data for {{ source_name }}: $ARGUMENTS

Reference data the upstream publishes for every caller alike (code lists, attribute
lists, report calendars) may ship inside the package under
`src/xippios/{{ package_dir }}/reference/` and is loaded with `xippios.core.reference`. It
comes from the upstream, never from memory. Not every source packages lists: one that
changes often stays a lookup task that calls upstream. A multi-tenant source (ADR-0014)
packages no tenant data, only reference lists that are the same for every tenant, if any.

1. Work on a branch `chore/reference-<thing>`.
2. Identify the upstream list endpoint for each list named in the arguments, using
   `docs/upstream/`; a wrapper for it belongs in `api/` (`/add-endpoint`).
3. Add the list to `LISTS` in `scripts/refresh_reference.py` (name as upstream calls it,
   fetcher through `api/`) and to `PACKAGED_LISTS` in `params.py`, then run the script
   from the repository root with whatever the list endpoints need in the environment
   (a key only where they take one): it writes `reference/<name>.json` with the fetch
   date and regenerates `enums.py`. Tables that are not plain value lists go under
   `reference/` as CSV with upstream column names, written by the same script.
4. Format the regenerated `enums.py`, in this order:
   `uv run ruff format src/xippios/{{ package_dir }}/enums.py && uv run ruff check --fix src/xippios/{{ package_dir }}/enums.py`.
   `render_str_enums` in xippios-core currently writes `__all__` unsorted on one line
   (ruff RUF022 and, past 100 characters, E501); formatting first leaves only the sort
   for `--fix`. The fix in core is https://github.com/xippios/sdk-core/pull/20; drop
   this step once a core release carries it.
5. Update the provenance table in `reference/README.md`: file, origin (endpoint or
   URL), fetch date.
6. If agents have no lookup task for this list yet, add one with `/add-endpoint` so they
   can look the codes up: one lookup task per list (`list_<thing>`), or the upstream's
   own lookup operation when it serves every list through one. A lookup over a packaged
   list returns the packaged data, it does not call upstream.
7. Run the checks and open the PR with the diff summarised: how many rows changed, any
   codes that disappeared (those may break existing fixtures or callers). Do not merge.
